OVERVIEW
Kevin presented for evaluation after sustaining a traumatic injury. Radiographs did not show any
obvious rib fractures or other major bony abnormalities. However, there was mild to moderate localized
swelling within the soft tissues along the caudal aspect of the right side of the chest wall. This swelling
may be associated with bruising, fluid accumulation, or inflammation from the recent trauma. Less
commonly, this appearance can be associated with infection or a developing inflammatory process.
Kevin received pain medication in the hospital and additional pain control was recommended for home
to help keep him comfortable while he recovers.
MEDICATIONS
See above for medication instructions.
Never use Advil, Motrin, Tylenol, their generic equivalents, or any other human painkiller in dogs and cats
as they are very toxic and potentially fatal.
VEG | Discharge Summary | 1
Give all medications exactly as prescribed, even if Kevin appears comfortable. Do not give additional
doses unless directed by a veterinarian.
MONITORING
Please contact your primary care veterinarian or VEG if you see any of the following:
Labored breathing, excessive panting, or persistent cough
Worsening swelling, redness, heat, or discharge along the right side of the chest
Pain, inability to get comfortable, or crying
Pale, white, gray, or blue gums
Persistent vomiting or diarrhea
Excessive sleepiness, weakness, or lethargy
Collapse or significant worsening of Kevin’s overall condition
Decreased appetite or inability to keep food/water down
Any other worsening or concerning changes
AT -HOME CARE
Keep Kevin calm and restrict strenuous activity while he recovers from his injury. Avoid running, jumping,
rough play, or activities that could cause additional trauma to the affected area.
Monitor the swelling along his right chest wall daily. It should not become progressively larger,
increasingly painful, red, hot, or develop abnormal discharge.
Encourage normal eating and drinking and monitor Kevin’s comfort and activity level closely.
FOLLOW-UP
Please follow up with Kevin’s primary care veterinarian for continued monitoring of his traumatic injury
and soft tissue swelling.
We will contact you with Kevin’s bloodwork results once they are available. If his signs fail to improve or
worsen despite current treatments, or if any other concerning signs arise, please follow up with your
primary care veterinarian or Veterinary Emergency Group. 
Thank you for entrusting Veterinary Emergency Group of H Street with your pet's care care. If you have
any questions, comments or concerns about your pet or the visit please feel free to reach out via email
(hstreet@veg.vet) or telephone (202) 794-8002.
