Presenting complaint: trauma, pain Patient History:Reason for today's visit: Right flank/abdominal trauma with painBackground Information: Kevin, a 
cat, presented after suspected blunt trauma to the right flank/abdomen yesterday when the owner reports a cleaning person swung a broom at him. 
Owner notes he is extremely painful over that area and has not been moving much tonight. Owner transported him tonight; he vocalized throughout 
the car ride. Owner reports he is generally a couch potato. Weight recorded as 5.4kg Medications: Gabapentin 50 mg PO given at approximately 
12:15 AM today; owner reports no improvement.Last time patient ate: Not specified; owner was asked about peeing and eating but no clear response 
provided.V/D/C/S: NonePU/PD: NonePrevention: Not specified.Vx Hx: Not up to date on vaccines.Other pet(s): Lives with 5 cats.Travel Hx: No recent 
travel reported.Medical Hx: History of urethral obstruction in 2021 treated at Dogs and Cats; has been fine since per owner.Focal pain on palpation of 
the right flank/abdominal region. Any evidence of rib fractures/trauma please speak to any and all abnormalities seen in films sent no masses or FF 
seen on POCUS
OBSERVATIONS :
Orthogonal views of the thorax and abdomen, 7 images.
THORAX:
The cardiovascular structures are normal in size and shape. 
Generalized mild thickening of the walls of the bronchial tree.  Otherwise the pulmonary parenchyma is unremarkable.
Normal width and opacity of the cranial mediastinum. 
Unremarkable pleural space.
No skeletal abnormalities are detected.
ABDOMEN:
The stomach is moderately distended with gas and heterogeneous soft tissue material.
The small intestine contains gas, fluid and occasional granular contents.  It is normally distributed in the abdomen and measures within normal limits 
in diameter throughout.
The colon contains gas and normally formed fecal material.
There is adequate abdominal serosal detail.
The spleen is moderately diffusely enlarged
The hepatic, and renal silhouettes are normal in size and shape.
The urinary bladder is mildly distended and has preserved homogeneous soft tissue opacity. 
Focal, mild to moderate and broad-based soft tissue swelling involving the caudal aspect of the right thoracic wall.  No evidence of gas inclusions or 
radiopaque foreign material within the area of soft tissue swelling causing partial effacement of visualization of the fascial planes.  Otherwise no 
musculoskeletal abnormalities are detected.
CONCLUSIONS:
1.  Mild to moderate focal subcutaneous swelling at the caudal aspect of the right thoracic wall, consistent with edema, cellulitis, hemorrhage or less 
likely phlegmonous changes.  If clinically indicated consider ultrasound for further evaluation.  No evidence of associated rib fractures, tearing of the 
intercostal musculature or rupture abdominal wall.
2.  Gastric and small intestinal contents may represent normal ingesta or less likely non-obstructive foreign material. There is no evidence of gastric 
outflow or small intestinal obstruction at this time point.  If clinically indicated fasted follow-up radiographs in 8 to 10 hours can be considered to 
monitor for normal intestinal transit.
Splenomegaly. Differentials include extramedullary hematopoiesis, splenitis as a result of a systemic or inflammatory response, or vascular 
congestion following sedation. Round cell infiltration can however not be ruled out and if this is of clinical concern, consider abdominal ultrasound +/- 
splenic FNA.
3.  Mild diffuse bronchial pattern, the primary differential is age-related changes, concurrent inflammatory/allergic bronchitis (feline asthma) cannot be 
ruled out.  Otherwise radiographically unremarkable thorax  
